Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  what do you think of when you hear the  words violence prevention seriously take  a second and think about that many of  you are probably thinking law  enforcement right well my hope is that  by the end of this talk healthcare comes  into your mind just as quickly as law  enforcement probably did if not even  faster so one question that some folks  often ask is why is violence so common  violence is a pretty widely agreed-upon  societal veiss we all know it's bad and  yet it's everywhere according to the  FBI's Uniform Crime reporting program in  2016  there are over 1.2 million violent  crimes committed in the u.s. over 17,000  of these resulted in deaths now that's  the size of the entire student  population here at Stanford so it's a  fair question why is violence so come  let me try and explain with the personal  story when I was 12 I moved into the  Sumner housing projects of Brooklyn New  York I was actually really excited about  this because there was a basketball  court right in front of my new apartment  I've been playing basketball for a while  and still wasn't very good but you see  all that would change now because I  could practice on this court  I actually remember going up to all my  friends and saying hey guys give me  about a year practice and I'm dunking on  everybody I was uh pretty Delusion with  back then yeah so one day I was out  there on the court shooting around and  as I said it wasn't very good so most of  those shots miss and on one of the  misses the ball bounced off the rim and  rolled to the side of the park another  kid picks it up and walks off so I run  up to him and say hey man hey that's uh  that's my ball and he keeps on walking  and before I know it I'm surrounded by  about 20 other kids all with their fists  clenched and eyebrows furrow and they  were yelling yelling yo yo what you  doing here snow what you doing here who  you with  I was a fresh-faced clueless  twelve-year-old kid I didn't know what  to say  I got beat up pretty badly that day and  as I eventually managed to push my way  out of the crowd of sneakers and fisting  ran home eyes full of tears I thought  the next time something like this  happens I need to have something to say  Who am I with who can defend me here and  that's how you end up befriended some  pretty sketchy characters that introduce  you to gangs guns and before you know it  you've been fully initiated into the  vicious cycle of violence I was lucky in  that I have an incredible month whenever  I screwed up she would put me in my  place it's funny really I'm a lot taller  than my mom didn't matter she would  stand up on top of a chair she had to  look down at me screaming at the top of  her lungs to make sure that I stayed on  the straight and narrow but  unfortunately there are a lot of other  folks in these kinds of communities that  don't have that influence and thus we  end up with this huge issue of violence  now once you ought to imagine yourselves  growing up in Sumner right along with me  to help you do that let's paint a  picture Sumner is actually a really  lively neighborhood there are tons of  beautiful parks and trees little kids  running around as they play on pretty  much every block now there is loud music  always blasting from someone's window  behind you  barbecues here you're surrounded by a  whole lot of happiness but also a whole  lot of violence growing up in Sumner you  saw gangs drugs fighting shooting  constant policing the whole night now  imagine the inevitable happens and  you get shot what happens next  someone calls 9 1 1 an ambulance rushes  down picks you up and brings you to the  nearest emergency department there  you're treated stabilized and thankfully  recovering in your hospital bed what do  you see around you all kinds of tubes  sticking in and out of your body  loud beeping sounds that never seem to  stop unfamiliar faces in these bright  white coats coming in and out and in and  out of your room what's going through  your mind in this moment probably  something like wow I almost died today I  may have never seen my parents siblings  boyfriend/girlfriend anyone again I  can't go back to this life someone  anyone help and in that moment you are  probably more receptive to intervention  than you've ever been before but that's  also keep in mind a huge window of  opportunity that law enforcement just  doesn't have but unfortunately in many  instances these patients are treated and  then sent right back to the same violent  environment that spawned those injuries  in the first place  with no means of preventing recurrence  so you may be asking ok but what can we  actually do for these folks inside of a  hospital well a lot actually I'm here at  Stanford for an MBA but I'm also a  fourth year medical student at Harvard  while there I worked with the violence  recovery program or the VRP at the  Brigham and Women's Hospital the VRP is  a hospital-based violence prevention  program that targets patients who are  victims and perpetrators of violence and  provides the resources necessary to stop  them from coming back to the hospital  now at the VRP this mainly took the form  of counseling but our counselors were  unique in that they each had their own  personal experiences with violence so  often that let me add that that was in  the very same neighborhoods that these  patients were from so whereas more  traditional counselors might have been  trained at graduate program these folks  are trained by life and that enabled  them to establish a much more personal  connection  with our patience but it doesn't need to  end there one can imagine also including  things like housing relocation job  training legal support educational  support really any number of services  that would be the most effective for a  given patient and the beautiful thing is  that these services often already exist  in the communities that these patients  are from and thus all a hospital based  program would need to do would be to  connect these patients to these services  during this window of opportunity  imagine the impact this could have if  skilled now there are a number of  similar programs across the country and  they're all housed under a larger  umbrella organization known as the  national network for hospital based  violence intervention programs or the  nnh VIP there are about 32 programs  within this network and they're all  doing great work but again just 32  programs there are far more than 32  hospitals in this country and even more  communities that struggle with violence  violence is a tremendous issue according  to the CDC violence is listed as the  second and third leading causes of death  among American youth second being  suicide and third homicide now that  place is it higher than any of the more  traditional medical disorders you can  probably think of cancer heart disease  diabetes all of that so for something  that has such a large impact on the  health and mortality of patients I find  it imperative that we in health care  address this so I want to ask you all to  consider three things and before I say  that I just want to stress this point  that violence is a public health issue  and we can't leave it to law enforcement  to deal with all alone so for those  three things what if you're a clinician  in the audience or watching online if  you work in a hospital that serves  communities struggling with violence  consider starting your own violence  prevention program it could go a long  way in improving the overall health of  your patient population to  if you're not a clinician maybe no one  or a hospital administrator a  policymaker really any decision-maker in  this space use those connections and  convince those folks to try and help  create more of these programs and three  if you're not a clinician and don't know  any decision-makers that's okay too at  the very least think back to the  question that we started this talk with  what do you think of when you hear the  words violence prevention I hope that  now you can appreciate the impact that  healthcare can have in reducing violence  and the next time you talk about  violence prevention I hope that  healthcare is one of the first things  that comes into your mind thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

